  "root_cause": "The bug is in get_host() using the module-level variable _default_host which was set once at import time from _settings (line: _default_host = _settings["host"]). Because _default_host is captured eagerly, subsequent calls to configure(...) that change _settings do not affect _default_host and get_host() continues to return the original import-time value.",
  "failure_mechanism": "Example scenario: module imported (so _default_host == 'localhost'). Later code calls configure(host='example.com') which updates _settings['host'] but does not change _default_host. A call to get_host() still returns 'localhost' even though get_settings() would show {'host': 'example.com', ...}. The cause-effect chain is: import sets _default_host -> configure mutates _settings but not _default_host -> get_host() returns stale _default_host.",
  "broken_invariant": "After calling configure(host=<new_host>), get_host() must return <new_host>. This invariant is violated because get_host() returned the original import-time host (e.g., 'localhost') instead of the configured value.",
  "fix_strategy": "Removed the eagerly-captured _default_host and introduced an explicit _DEFAULT_SETTINGS constant. get_host() now reads the current host from the mutable _settings dict so changes made by configure(...) are immediately visible. reset_settings() was changed to mutate the existing _settings dict (clear() + update(DEFAULTS)) instead of rebinding the global name, preserving any external references to the settings dict. These changes ensure get_host() reflects configure(...) and that reset_settings() reliably restores defaults.",
